1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
 Cookies, localStorage and sessionStorage.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,600
 Before cookies and localStorage and all of it can make sense,

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,360
 you need to know first of all why a browser has to remember anything at all.

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,320
 The web runs on HTTP, and HTTP is stateless.

5
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:16,480
 That means that every request your browser sends stands on its own.

6
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,760
 The server doesn't remember the request that came before it.

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,720
 So if you log into your shopping website, that's one request.

8
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:26,720
 If you click on My Orders, that's a brand new request on its own.

9
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:32,320
 So, as the server knows that it's you. Think about a cloak room. We have something similar in my

10
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:37,680
 school where you hand over your jacket or your coat and they give you a ticket with a name on it.

11
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:42,160
 They don't remember your face because I mean there's a lot of people. But when you come back,

12
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:47,280
 you show them the ticket and they use that number to find a jacket or your coat. A cookie kind of

13
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:53,440
 works like that ticket. After you log in, the server gives your browser a cookie with a session

14
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:59,280
 ID in it. And your browser shows that cookie on every request after that. So the first place that

15
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:03,680
 a website can keep something in your browser is in a cookie. If you use a website, you see things

16
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:09,520
 like a Set-Cookie, a set necessary cookie, and all of that. A cookie is a small piece of text,

17
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:14,480
 usually a name and a value that the server asks your browser to hold on to. The server usually

18
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:20,160
 sets it with an header on the response called Set-Cookie. After that code cookie has been set,

19
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:22,560
 every time your browser sends a request to that site,

20
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:25,280
 it sends the cookie back automatically in a cookie header.

21
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:27,600
 Each cookie can hold about 4 kilobytes,

22
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:29,680
 and it's good to keep them very small

23
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:31,680
 because they travel with every request.

24
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:33,360
 Now, how long can a cookie live?

25
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,600
 If the server doesn't give the cookie an expiry,

26
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,040
 then it's a session cookie.

27
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:39,600
 It's meant to be deleted when you close the browser.

28
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,200
 I say meant to because a lot of browsers

29
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,440
 can restore your tab when you reopen them,

30
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,440
 and the session cookie can come back with them.

31
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:49,680
 If the server, however, sets Expires or Max-Age,

32
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:51,680
 Then it's a persistent cookie.

33
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:54,520
 It stays until that time that is set.

34
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:57,280
 And JavaScript on a page can also read and write cookies

35
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:59,200
 through document.cookie.

36
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:01,720
 Now in my e-commerce store, the login

37
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:03,600
 lives in a cookie like this.

38
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:07,440
 The cookie itself only holds a random ID, a session ID.

39
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,600
 The server keeps the real details about you

40
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:12,160
 and uses that ID to look them up,

41
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:14,920
 like the cloakroom number and your jacket.

42
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:18,200
 The second place a website can keep things in your browser

43
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:19,980
 is localStorage.

44
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:22,440
 localStorage is the reason why maybe you add something

45
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:24,200
 to your cart and close the browser,

46
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:25,840
 and when you come back and reopen it,

47
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,520
 you still see the stuff in your cart.

48
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:29,800
 localStorage is a simple store of key and value

49
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,040
 that live in your browser for one website.

50
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:34,360
 It only stores strings.

51
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:37,840
 So for a cart, a shopping cart, you

52
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:40,920
 turn it into strings with JSON stringify.

53
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:44,080
 And when you want to get it back, you get it back with JSON.parse.

54
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,320
 Most browsers give you about five megabytes per website.

55
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:49,320
 But it varies.

56
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:51,360
 So you don't want something that is also too big

57
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:52,760
 in your localStorage.

58
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:54,080
 And by website, I mean the origin,

59
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:57,620
 which is the protocol, the domain name, the ports altogether.

60
00:02:57,620 --> 00:03:00,800
 localStorage stays until your code removes it,

61
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:03,400
 or until the user cleared their browser data.

62
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:05,600
 Closing the tab or even closing the old browser

63
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,840
 doesn't clear your localStorage.

64
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:09,340
 But there are two exceptions.

65
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:11,400
 In a private or incognito window,

66
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,680
 it's usually deleted when you close the window.

67
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:17,980
 Also, localStorage data is never sent to the server.

68
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,900
 If the server needs your cards, your code has to send it.

69
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:23,060
 The third place is the sessionStorage.

70
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:25,460
 It's the reason why an R field checkout form

71
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:27,500
 can disappear when you close the tab.

72
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,060
 sessionStorage has the same method as localStorage,

73
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:31,980
 setItem, getItem, removeItem.

74
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:35,260
 The difference between sessionStorage and localStorage

75
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:37,520
 is that sessionStorage belongs to one tab.

76
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:39,600
 If you refresh the page, it's still there.

77
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:41,940
 But if you close the tab, it's gone.

78
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:43,960
 If you open the same e-commerce website by yourself

79
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,400
 in the second tab, that second tab

80
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,320
 gets its own separate sessionStorage.

81
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:48,860
 So sessionStorage is good for things

82
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:51,660
 that only matter in this tab right now,

83
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:53,460
 like an app-filled form,

84
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:55,760
 or search field tab that someone picks.

85
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:58,520
 You obviously don't want to keep very important things

86
00:03:58,520 --> 00:03:59,960
 in sessionStorage.

87
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:03,200
 Now, who can read Cookie's localStorage sessionStorage?

88
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:05,480
 Another big difference between all three of them

89
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:07,040
 is who gets to read them.

90
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:08,460
 Cookies go to the server.

91
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:11,120
 The browser attaches them to every request

92
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:13,800
 that matches the cookies, domain, and path.

93
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,600
 localStorage and sessionStorage never leave the browser

94
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:18,280
 by itself.

95
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:21,280
 The server only see them if your code sends them.

96
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:24,000
 But inside the browser, any JavaScript running on your page

97
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,920
 can read your localStorage and sessionStorage.

98
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:27,460
 That includes your own code, but it also

99
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:30,040
 includes every third-party script that you've added,

100
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:32,540
 like maybe an analytic script or a chat widget.

101
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:35,920
 Cookies can be read by JavaScript through document.cookie.

102
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:37,960
 Except, of course, the server explicitly

103
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:39,920
 marks them HttpOnly.

104
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:43,160
 So based on the comparison, cookies are small,

105
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:45,760
 and they go to the server with every matching request.

106
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:49,520
 JavaScript can read them unless they are set as HttpOnly.

107
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:51,920
 localStorage and sessionStorage, on the other hand,

108
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:53,560
 holds more than a cookie.

109
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:56,160
 But they stay in the browser, they do not get sent to the server,

110
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:58,920
 and every script on the page can read them.

111
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:01,360
 localStorage stays until it's cleared,

112
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:03,760
 but sessionStorage lives with a tab.

113
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:05,880
 So if you close the tab, sessionStorage is gone.

114
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:10,820
 So in our online shop, the login is most likely

115
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:13,860
 an HTTP-only cookie, and the guest's cart

116
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:17,440
 is probably in localStorage, while the half-filled checkout

117
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:20,520
 form is probably in sessionStorage.

